WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:03.719
I’ve been using the Apple Watch Series 5
since it came out in September, which is about

00:00:03.719 --> 00:00:07.600
4 months ago, and in this video, I want to
share my thoughts on the differences I’ve

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.559
noticed compared to my old Series 4 Apple
Watch, as well as my final conclusion on whether

00:00:12.559 --> 00:00:17.130
it was worth upgrading to the Series 5, and
the features I believe could be coming to

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.230
the Series 6 watch this September.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:24.020
Now my old Series 4 was actually the Gold
stainless steel model with the gold chain

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:27.840
link band, and that’s the exact combination
I chose on the Series 5.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.360
I’ve compared both of them very closely,
and there is literally no physical differences

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:37.550
at all except for the little engraving on
the back that says Series 5 instead of Series

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:38.550
4.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.070
So they’re basically identical.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:42.630
Now why in the world would I make this upgrade?

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:46.390
Well, the most obvious reason is that we run
this YouTube channel and we make a lot of

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:51.420
Apple-related videos, so we’ve gotta have
the latest Apple products to test and review.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.640
But if we toss that aside, the main reason
I actually wanted to upgrade was the always-on

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:56.640
display.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.921
Yes, the Series 5 technically does get the
new Compass feature which you can’t get

00:01:00.921 --> 00:01:04.260
on the Series 4, and I’ll talk about that
in just a minute,

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:10.070
It also gets 32GB of storage versus only 16
on the Series 4, but that honestly hasn’t

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:14.440
mattered much to me because I never even hit
the 16GB limit on my Series 4.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:19.460
There’s a reason why there were so little
changes and differences with the Series 5.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:24.840
The Apple Watch has already almost hit its
peak in terms of design, features and software.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:29.450
The performance of the Series 4 was already
great, the new display was a huge change that

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:31.760
makes it look much better.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.390
The feature-set is already so vast, that it’s
hard to think of more features that can be

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:37.440
packed into the Apple Watch.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.770
The Series 4 was already the best smartwatch
you could get, and it’s easy to see that

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:46.380
there wasn’t much left to improve on with
the Series 5, except of course, the always-on

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:47.380
display.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:50.660
So here’s why the always-on display is such
a big deal.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:54.840
For years, it’s been missing, and a lot
of people have been asking for it.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.869
The main reasons are due to functionality,
being able to see the time, your messages,

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:04.900
the temperature or any of your complications
at a glance without having to raise it or

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.280
tap it with your finger.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:12.390
To some people, that might sound ridiculous,
but that little bit of extra convenience actually

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:13.470
adds a lot.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:18.250
For myself personally, that does help, but
I also care about the style side of the always-on

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:19.250
display.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:23.760
Previously, with the Series 4, I would at
times swap out my Apple Watch for a nice regular

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.730
watch when going out to a nice restaurant,
a wedding, or events, even though I would

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.080
lose out on the obvious smart functionality.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:35.510
That’s because for many years, the watch
has been seen as the finishing touch to an

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:37.840
outfit, like for a suit and tie, for example.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:43.239
Have a solid block of black on the face of
the Series 4 really seemed to kill that finishing

00:02:43.239 --> 00:02:44.239
touch.

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:49.310
But with the Series 5 and the always-on display,
your watch face is always lit up so it looks

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.129
much more like a normal watch.

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:55.599
Of course, this might sound farfetched to
some people, but due to the always-on display

00:02:55.599 --> 00:02:59.319
alone, I’ve noticed that I’ve stopped
wearing some of my other watches altogether

00:02:59.319 --> 00:03:04.180
because the Series 5 actually looks good and
I’m not the only one who thinks so.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:08.230
You’ll find a lot of different Series 5
Reviews on Youtube, where people are switching

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:13.829
back to the Apple Watch for the always-on
display feature alone, so that’s gotta say

00:03:13.829 --> 00:03:14.829
something!

00:03:14.829 --> 00:03:17.989
One thing I also like is that different faces
act differently when you turn the display

00:03:17.989 --> 00:03:18.989
off.

00:03:18.989 --> 00:03:23.099
Some turn a white background black, and others
leave an outline of the time so it’s clearly

00:03:23.099 --> 00:03:24.919
visible and easy to read.

00:03:24.919 --> 00:03:28.549
But other than that, there really isn’t
much of a difference between the Series 4

00:03:28.549 --> 00:03:29.549
and Series 5.

00:03:29.549 --> 00:03:33.870
I haven’t noticed any differences at all
in performance, functionality or anything

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:37.339
else, but I’ve actually been getting very
impressive battery life considering the display

00:03:37.339 --> 00:03:41.680
is always-on, and I always have it set to
the brightest setting as well.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:46.519
I used to get to around 50-55% battery life
at the end of the day, and with the Series

00:03:46.519 --> 00:03:52.900
5, it’s now at around 35%, so losing 20-25%
battery life is a good sacrifice to be able

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:54.870
to have an always-on display.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:59.400
The only time the Series 5 died on me was
when I went on a long 6 hour hike and I had

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.349
all of the workout activity tracking features
enabled.

00:04:02.349 --> 00:04:04.400
But other than that, it’s been great!

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.930
The cool thing about the Series 5 is that
the battery itself is actually bigger, so

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:12.510
you can disable always-on in the settings
and get even more battery life when you don’t

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:14.150
want to use that feature.

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:19.209
Now let’s talk about the Compass, the feature
that’s not available on the Series 4.

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:23.200
Even though I have the gold chain link band,
the compass is working perfectly fine.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:29.910
I personally haven’t really used it because
you really only need it when you’re lost,

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:34.950
and most hikes have trails that you stick
to for that reason.

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:40.070
However, the compass can actually be utilized
by other apps, like the Maps app, for example.

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:45.300
This enables the watch to show you which direction
you’re facing, which can be really helpful

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:46.570
with navigation.

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:51.090
And Apple should be allowing third-party developers
to make use of the compass to add this functionality

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:53.160
into running apps and other apps like that.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:57.570
Now I know the Series 4 also supports the
same features that you get with watchOS 6,

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:01.470
but I’ve gotta say that I’m really liking
the new ambient noise feature.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:06.020
It’s actually been quite useful for a couple
of the videos we’ve done on this channel.

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:11.350
For example, we used it to show off the difference
in speaker volume in our 15 vs 16” macbook

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:15.120
pro comparison video, and we used it again
when we compared the volume of the fan noise

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.110
between them when at maximum load, so this
is probably one of my favorite new features

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:20.810
with watchos 6.

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:25.460
Now before I give you my final answer on if
upgrading was worth it or not, I want to mention

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:28.840
the features I would really like to see on
the Apple Watch Series 6.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:33.590
First off, I think it would be really cool
if Apple created a round version of the Apple

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:34.590
Watch.

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:37.830
If you think about, Apple’s done basically
everything they possibly can with the current

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:42.310
Apple Watch design, so a round option would
be great and help make it look even more like

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:43.780
a normal classy watch.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:48.400
I’d also like Apple to improve on the battery
tech even more so that we would have an option

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.880
to not have the display dim when you’re
not using it.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:55.780
I actually avoid some watch faces because
in always-on mode, it gets so dim that it’s

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:59.350
kind of hard to tell the time, so I ended
up lifting my wrist anyway, so it would be

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:01.140
great if they’d be brighter.

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:05.430
There were some rumors of blood-glucose monitoring
coming to the Apple Watch, and I think that

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:09.790
would be a huge accomplishment for Apple,
making life a lot easier for people who need

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:10.790
to check their blood sugar.

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:11.790
Apart from that, there’s really not that
much features I’d want on the Apple Watch

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:12.790
because it basically already has everything
I could think of.

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:15.750
On the software side, I think it would be
awesome if Apple created a Watch Face store,

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:19.750
where third-party companies or people could
create custom watch faces than you can purchase

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:20.750
for a price.

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:24.660
This would create a new profit stream for
Apple and make a lot of people happy by having

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:26.590
a larger base of watch faces.

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:30.790
Now the final question, do I regret upgrading?

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:35.640
Well, Kinda, sorta, yes, but at the same time
no.

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:40.150
If we weren’t making YouTube videos, I would
not have the Series 5 right now because I

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:42.170
wouldn’t have paid the money to upgrade.

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:46.461
It’s simply just too similar and I really
haven’t noticed any other difference other

00:06:46.461 --> 00:06:48.610
than the always-on display.

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:53.620
But at the same time I really, really, like
the always on display.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:58.430
The only reason to regret upgrading is the
extra money spent, and four months later I

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:06.460
still feel kinda bad about it, but ultimately
if I ask myself would I trade back to a series

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:11.810
4 right now and get the price difference back
then no I wouldn’t.

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:15.020
I really like the series 5 and the always
on display.

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:19.770
And for brand new Apple Watch customers, I
would absolutely recommend going for the Series

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:24.320
5 over the Series 3 because of all of the
great feature changes that came with the Series

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:27.060
4 plus the few new ones with 5.

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:32.460
Amazon does still have the Series 4 available
renewed for $330, but I honestly think paying

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:37.510
$85 more for a brand new Series 5 is definitely
worth it.

00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:41.060
Whichever Apple Watch model you choose, we’ll
have some affiliate links down below that

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:43.540
you can use if you appreciate the information.

